.TH CC 1
.SH NAME
cc \- C compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cc
[ option ] ... file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cc
is the VENIX C compiler.
It normally takes one or more C language
source files and compiles and loads them to produce an executable program.
Optionally, it can stop compilation at the assembler or object
code level, or take assembler, object files, or object libraries, and produce
executable programs.
.PP
.B cc
accepts several types of file names as arguments:
.PP
File names ending with `.c' are taken to be C source programs; they are
compiled, and each object program is left on the file whose name is that
of the source with `.o' substituted for `.c'.
The `.o' file is normally
deleted, however, if a single C program is compiled and loaded all at one
go.
.PP
In the same way, arguments whose names end with `.s' are taken to be
assembly source programs and are assembled, producing a `.o' file.
.PP
Other files are taken as object-code modules or libraries of such modules
produced by
.BR ar (1).
Standard VENIX libraries in
.B /lib
and
.B /usr/lib
can be
referred to using the shorthand provided by the
.B \-l
flag (see below).
.PP
The following options are interpreted by
.BR cc .
It is also possible to include
loader flags on the same line.
See
.BR ld (1)
for load-time options.
(Note also
that for normal use the loader
.B \-l
flag, used to load an additional
library, must come at the very
.I end
of the command line given
.BR cc .)
.TP
.B \-c
Suppress the loading phase of the compilation, and force an
object file to be produced even if only one program is compiled.
.TP
.B \-p
Arrange for the compiler to produce code which counts the
number of times each routine is called; also, if loading takes
place, replace the standard startup routine by one which
automatically calls
.BR monitor (3)
at the start and arranges to write
out a
.I mon.out
file at normal termination of execution of the
object program.
An execution profile can then be generated by
use of
.BR prof (1).
.TP
.B \-O
Invoke an object-code optimizer.
.TP
.B \-f
For systems with 8087 floating point chips, generate code which
uses 8087 floating point instructions.
If this option is not
specified, a floating-point simulator is used and 8087 instructions
are not generated.
Do not use this option if an 8087 chip is not
present
(RAINBOW/VENIX only).
.TP
.B \-f
For systems without hardware floating-point, generate object
code which handles floating-point constants and loads the object
program with a floating-point simulator.
Do not use if the
hardware is present.
(VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only.)
.TP
.B \-S
Compile the named C programs, and leave the assembler-language
output on corresponding files suffixed `.s'.
.TP
.B \-P
Run only the macro preprocessor and place the result for each
`.c' file in a corresponding `.i' file that has no `#' lines in it.
.TP
.BI "\-o " output
Name the final output file
.IR output .
If this option is used the file
.B a.out
will be left undisturbed.
.TP
.B \-z
Generate code that checks for stack overflow upon subroutine
entry.
This option is also passed on to the loader, unless the
.B \-c
flag (suppress loader) option is given and object modules
only are produced.
In the latter case, the user must be sure to
specify a
.B \-z
when the loader is run on the object modules; a
.B \-z
specified for compilation only is ineffective.
See
.BR ld (1)
for the
.B \-z
flag effects on the loader.
(RAINBOW/VENIX only.)
.TP
.B \-m
Program is to be ``code-mapped.''
This is only done to run
unusually large programs; see documentation in the
.IR "Programming Guide" .
This flag must be used both when compiling and
linking programs (similar to the
.B \-z
flag); it is passed to the
assembler and the loader.
(VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only.)
.TP
.BI \-D name=def
.PD 0
.TP
.BI \-D name
.PD
Define the
.I name
to the preprocessor, as if by `#define'.
If no
definition is given, the name is defined as 1.
.TP
.BI \-U name
Remove any initial definition of
.IR name .
.TP
.BI \-I dir
`#include' files whose names do not begin with `/' are always
sought first in the directory of the
.I file
argument, then in directories
named in
.B \-I
options, then in
.BR /usr/include .
.TP
.BI \-B string
Find substitute compiler passes in the files named
.I string
with the
suffixes cpp, c0, c1 and c2.
If
.I string
is empty, use a standard
backup version.
This is only used for debugging or testing new
passes.
(VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only.)
.TP
.B \-t[p012]
Find only the designated compiler passes in the files whose
names are constructed by a
.B \-B
option.
In the absence of a
.B \-B
option, the
.I string
is taken to be
.BR /usr/c/ .
(VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only.)
.PP
Any and all object modules and libraries given, together with the results
of any compilations specified, are loaded (in the order given) to produce
an executable program with name
.BR a.out .
.PP
The loader is called in the following way:
.IP
ld \-X \fIstartoff args\fR \-lc
.PP
where
.I startoff
is one of the possible startoff object files shown below;
.I args
are any loader arguments and files specified by the user (.c files are
given .o extensions) in the order the user gave them; and
.B \-lc
specifies
the standard C library.
.SH FILES
.PD 0
.TP 1.4i
file.c
C source file
.TP
file.i
preprocessed file
.TP
file.s
assembly source file
.TP
file.o
object file
.TP
a.out
executable output from
.B cc
.TP
/lib/cpp
preprocessor
.TP
/lib/c0
compiler for
.B cc
.TP
/lib/c1
second compiler pass (VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only)
.TP
/lib/fc1
floating-point compiler (VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only)
.TP
/lib/copt
optional optimizer (RAINBOW/VENIX only)
.TP
/lib/c2
optional optimizer (VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only)
.TP
/lib/crt0.o
runtime startoff
.TP
/lib/mcrt0.o
startoff for profiling
.TP
/lib/fcrt0.o
startoff for floating-point interpretation (VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only)
.TP
/lib/fmcrt0.o
startoff for profiling w/floating-point (VENIX/11 and PRO/VENIX only)
.TP
/lib/libc.a
standard library, see
.BR intro (3)
.TP
/usr/include
standard directory for `#include' files
.PD
.SH EXAMPLES
Compile
.I prog.c
to produce an executable
.BR a.out :
.IP
cc prog.c
.PP
Compile
.I prog.c
to produce an object file
.I prog.o
.IP
cc \-c prog.c
.PP
Compile
.I prog.c
and link with math library:
.IP
cc prog.c \-lm
.SH "SEE ALSO"
B. W. Kernighan and D. M. Ritchie,
.IR "The C Programming Language" ,
Prentice-Hall, 1978
.br
``C Language'' in the
.IR "Programming Guide" .
.br
monitor(3), prof(1), adb(1), ld(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The diagnostics produced by C itself are intended to be largely self-explanatory.
Occasional messages may be produced by the assembler or
loader.
See
.BR as (1)
and
.BR ld (1)
for these errors.
.PP
The following errors may be produced by various stages of the C compiler
(explanations are given as deemed necessary):
.TP
.B XXX?
Can not create output file
.IR XXX .
The file may not exist, or may
exist and not be writable, or the directory in which it lies may
not be writable
.TP 1.4i
.B "0-length row: XXX"
.TP
.B "XXX: actuals too long"
Arguments passed when invoking macro function are
too long
.TP
.B "Arg count"
Compiler pass called with wrong number of command line arguments
.TP
.B "\-B overwrites earlier option"
.TP
.B "bad formal: XXX"
Illegal argument used when declaring macro function
.TP
.B "Bad func. storage class"
.TP
.B "bad include syntax"
Bad `#include' usage
.TP
.B "Bad structure/union/enum name"
.TP
.B "Bad type for field"
Illegal type declaration in bitfield
.TP
.B "Binary expression botch"
Internal error
.TP
.B "Botch in outcode"
Internal error
.TP
.B "Break/continue error"
Use of `break' or `continue' not within bounds of
`while', `for', etc.
.TP
.B "C error: const"
.TP
.B "Call of non-function"
Attempt to call something which is not a function
.TP
.B "Can't create XXX"
Can't create file: perhaps no write permission in
directory?
.TP
.B "Can't find include file XXX"
.TP
.B "Can't find XXX"
.TP
.B "Case not in switch"
Use of `case' statement outside of `switch'
.TP
.B "Can't create temp"
Can't create temporary storage file in /tmp
.TP
.B "compiler botch: call"
Internal error
.TP
.B "Compiler error (length)"
Internal error
.TP
